00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:58.880
The S&amp;P 500 closed at 7,674, up 4 tenths of a percent. The NASDAQ Composite closed at 26,180, also up 4 tenths of a percent. The TSX Composite closed at 36,620, up 7 tenths of a percent, and more than 250 points. Banking and mining names led the Canadian advance, while investors set aside fresh trade tension.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:12.080
The portfolio sits at $277 cumulative across 52 trades, with matched window alpha of negative 1.7% versus the NASDAQ, across one rules based trade.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:22.640
Rate decisions and earnings remain the next focal points. With the broader tape stable, let us move to today's simulated trade and how we are applying catalyst rules right now.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.860
Judging a FinTech stock before purchase starts with two core checks on revenue quality and competitive mode.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.940
Durable fee income matters more than one time gains when tariff friction can raise funding costs.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:46.540
Screen for companies whose net interest margin or take rate stayed steady through the last two rate cycles. Compare that margin against the top three peers using quarterly filings.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:54.800
Historical data shows this filter worked best in 2022 and 2023, when rate volatility exposed weak balance sheets.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:07.080
The main risk is overweating headline growth, while credit losses appear later. Pull the last four quarters from your broker screener or the SEC Edgar database and run the comparison in under 10 minutes.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:16.180
Before we see how that plays out, here is what yesterday's energy policy trade taught us. This is a simulated trade for educational purposes only.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:29.460
No real money is involved, and this is not financial advice. The pick is Microsoft on the NASDAQ. The catalyst is the confirmed arrival of first production in video Vera Rubin chips at Microsoft data centers.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:38.620
Price action sits above the 50 day moving average with volume near the 20 day average. nearest support rests at the 20 day moving average.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:54.360
A 6% stop loss caps the maximum loss if rollout news fades. The target range is 3 to 6% over 5 sessions. Confidence is medium because the catalyst is confirmed yet sector rotation into broader tech remains uncertain.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:01.420
The invalidation trigger is any public statement that the Vera Rubin rollout timeline slips beyond the current quarter.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:09.880
This pick shows how to turn a single executive quote into a rules based entry while respecting the volume filters we already have in place.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:18.380
We will track the position through Friday. Last week's hold was QEC.TO entered at 27 cents on the Monday open.

